ROAST.PET WEIRD BUILDER PROJECTS =E2=80=94 2026-09-20 13:24 Asia/Phnom_Penh

This pass screened 25+ candidates across pet CV/pose, browser video,
WebAR, image cleanup, personalized print generation, QR cards,
captions and talking-avatar tooling, and removed projects already
surfaced in earlier runs. Three cleared the no-padding bar.

1) WEBMOTION =E2=80=94 REUSE NOW / BENCHMARK AS PRIMARY BROWSER RENDERER
URL: https://github.com/superhq-ai/webmotion
Creator: superhq-ai
Activity: active/current; 106 commits surfaced today
License: MIT

What it solves: deterministic browser-native video composition without
headless Chrome, FFmpeg or a render farm. Everything visible is a pure
function of the current frame, so preview, seeking and export use the
same frame clock. It can use real DOM/HTML, canvas, Three.js glTF and
frame-exact WebCodecs video, then export MP4 client-side.

Why Roast.pet cares: this is a stronger architectural fit than
building our own compositor. A roast can remain ordinary
HTML/CSS/SVG/pet layers during authoring, while the same composition
becomes deterministic video. It also provides an html-in-canvas
backend, WebCodecs clip decoding and audio folded into export.

Install/demo: git clone https://github.com/superhq-ai/webmotion && cd
webmotion && npm install && npm run demo.
Chromium/WebCodecs/OffscreenCanvas for export; Three.js/mp4box are
optional modules for 3D/video.

Evidence: 106 commits, live scrub/export demo, unit
tests/typecheck/build commands, explicit deterministic rendering
architecture, MIT license.

Code/work replaced: potentially most of our browser renderer, frame
clock, DOM rasterization, WebCodecs encode path, video decode path and
preview/export synchronization.

Integration difficulty: MEDIUM
Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM; small project, but architecture is
modular and MIT.

Exact experiment: recreate the canonical 15-second Roast.pet podium
roast with pet RGBA, mouth/eye states, animated captions, TTS, sting
and one imported video clip. Export 9:16 MP4 on desktop Chromium and
weak laptop. Seek to 20 random frames and pixel-compare preview
against exported frames. If deterministic and fast enough, make
WebMotion the renderer adapter and stop implementing our own WebCodecs
stack.

VERDICT: REUSE NOW / BENCHMARK.

2) EDOF =E2=80=94 REUSE NOW FOR PRINT/CARD ARTIFACT GENERATION
URL: https://github.com/DavidSchobl/edof
PyPI: https://pypi.org/project/edof/
Creator: David Schobl
Activity/release: current v4.4.0 surfaced; actively maintained
License: MIT

What it solves: programmatic + visually editable personalized print
documents. The Python model has millimetre-positioned text, images,
shapes, tables and QR codes, vector PDF/PNG/JPEG/TIFF/SVG exports,
typed variables and a WYSIWYG editor. Its 3D Batch engine fills any
property across multiple page templates and emits per-row files or one
multipage document.

Why Roast.pet cares: this is almost exactly the deterministic
personalized-card layer we need. One order row can drive pet art,
owner name, roast headline, QR target, card front/back, color accents
and output filenames. QR is a first-class object rather than something
we need to bolt on. The same template is editable visually and
callable from Python.

Install/API: pip install edof; pip install edof[qr] for QR generation;
edof[pyqt6] for editor. Core requires Python 3.10+, Pillow and numpy;
optional GPU effects use moderngl with CPU fallback.

Evidence: v4.4.0 includes batch export, external-source bundles, image
recompression, live row preview, vector PDF, WYSIWYG stability, QR
objects and numerous export formats. The package explicitly supports
card decks/posters and data-driven output.

Code/work replaced: most custom card-template engine, QR placement,
print geometry, multi-format export, batch variable substitution and
potentially the internal card editor.

Integration difficulty: LOW-MEDIUM
Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM. MIT and active, but only two GitHub
stars: pin a known version and snapshot templates.

Exact experiment: build ONE Roast.pet order template containing front,
inside/back and QR. Feed 20 fake orders with different pet PNGs,
names, roast headlines and URLs. Export 300-DPI PNG plus print PDF.
Verify dimensions/bleed, QR scanability after print-scale
rasterization, text overflow, transparency and deterministic
regeneration.

VERDICT: REUSE NOW.

3) PNPINK =E2=80=94 FORK/COPY PRODUCTION PRINT MECHANISM; COMPARE AGAINST E=
DOF
URL: https://github.com/xoellijo/pnpink
Creator: xoellijo
Activity: active beta; 90 commits
License: MIT

What it solves: mature variable-data production from editable Inkscape
SVG templates. CSV/Google Sheets rows map into IDs such as
name/photo/title/art. It supports precise fit/anchor placement, rich
text/icons, bleed, margins, cutting marks, duplex front/back
generation, mirrored print layouts, reusable export presets, PDF/X
CMYK, ICC profiles, PNG/JPEG/TIFF/WebP/SVG/PS/EPS and large-output
splitting.

Why Roast.pet cares: EDOF is cleaner as a Python-native engine, but
PnPInk has deeper real-print-production machinery. In particular,
duplex/mirrored layouts, bleed/cut marks, PDF/X/CMYK/ICC and keeping
source output as editable SVG are exactly the boring print details we
should not rediscover.

Install: Inkscape 1.2+ on Windows/macOS/Linux; install extension using
pnpink installer. Template is ordinary Inkscape SVG; data can be CSV
or Google Sheets.

Evidence: 90 commits, current beta, documentation/examples, portable
project packages and stated high-throughput paths. Project recommends
pinning the version used for production because DSL is still evolving.

Code/work replaced: print-prepress logic, duplex sheet layout,
bleed/crop marks, color-profile/PDF/X handling and variable-data
template generation.

Integration difficulty: MEDIUM because it brings Inkscape into the worker i=
mage.
Maintenance risk: MEDIUM; active beta and DSL can change. Pin the
binary/version.

Exact experiment: take the SAME 20 fake orders from the EDOF test and
reproduce them in PnPInk. Compare final PDF dimensions, font fidelity,
bleed/crop marks, CMYK/PDF/X readiness, template-authoring speed and
headless automation friction. Pick one canonical print engine; do not
maintain both.

VERDICT: FORK/COPY MECHANISM / BENCHMARK AGAINST EDOF.

ADJACENCY / SEARCH NOTES

I also checked current pet-pose work. Ultralytics' Dog-Pose dataset
now exposes 8,476 images with 24 dog keypoints including paws, knees,
elbows, tail base/tip, ear bases/tips, nose, chin, eyes, withers and
throat. It is richer than the 17-point AP10K path surfaced earlier,
but the underlying Stanford Dogs/ImageNet imagery is
research-restricted, and the Ultralytics config/code is AGPL-3.0.
Therefore it is useful as a research benchmark/schema but NOT a clean
production dependency for Roast.pet without separate licensing. Do not
casually train a commercial model on it.

Other candidates reviewed but not promoted included ClearMatte
Studio/BiRefNet browser matting (too duplicative of Alphaveil), Rust
BiRefNet REST matting (generic), multiple MindAR card/video demos (we
already have stronger AR packaging/recording finds), Talking Pets
(interesting local Kokoro/VOICEVOX glue but not pet-animation-specific
enough), old CSV/SVG card makers (superseded by EDOF/PnPInk), and
generic business-card editors.

TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES
1. Browser frame clock + DOM/canvas/video compositor + WebCodecs
export before benchmarking WebMotion.
2. Personalized card layout engine from scratch before benchmarking EDOF.
3. QR placement/generation inside print artifacts.
4. Print-prepress machinery: duplex layout, bleed/cut marks, PDF/X,
CMYK and ICC handling.
5. Separate preview/export implementations for video or print; both
should derive from the same canonical scene/template.

MOST SURPRISING HOBBY PROJECT
EDOF. Two GitHub stars hides a Python document engine with a WYSIWYG
editor, QR objects, typed variables, multi-template batch generation,
vector PDF and per-row export. It is unusually close to Roast.pet's
physical-card compiler.

FASTEST INTEGRATION WIN
EDOF: pip install edof[qr], create one front/back card template, feed
one fake pet PNG + roast + QR URL, and see whether the resulting PDF
is already production-grade. This can answer the build-vs-reuse
question in an afternoon.

ONE REPO/AUTHOR WORTH FOLLOWING
superhq-ai/webmotion. The deterministic frame-as-pure-function
abstraction is exactly the right boundary for agent-edited Roast.pet
media, and the repo is active with 106 commits despite only
single-digit stars.

ARCHITECTURE UPDATE
The clean split now looks like:

pet.json + performance.json + roast/order data
  -> WebMotion =3D screen/social/video compiler
  -> EDOF or PnPInk =3D physical print compiler
  -> MindAR/QR =3D interactive delivery adapter
  -> Etsy/Printify =3D commerce adapter

That is preferable to one giant media pipeline. Roast.pet owns pet
identity, comedy/performance, QA and order data; OSS compilers turn
the same durable state into video, print and AR artifacts.
